// GitHub issue #451: the kernel's lock, as a type rather than as six
// anonymous integers.
//
// Six containers carried `private mutex: i32` and a guard that emitted no
// instruction. The integer was never checked to be a lock, was never the
// width an atomic operates on, and said nothing about what it protected.
// This is the one definition all of them share.
//
// WHY A STRUCT AND NOT A `usize`
//
// Three things, none of which a bare integer can do:
//
//   1. The width is structural. An atomic exchange in this language is
//      usize-wide; a 4-byte field would have had the exchange write over
//      whatever sat beside it. `Mutex` is usize-sized by construction, so
//      that class of bug cannot be reintroduced by a field declaration.
//   2. `*Mutex` is a type a reader can check. GitHub issue #396 is that a
//      slot address, a payload address and a lock word are all `usize`;
//      this is one of them saying which it is.
//   3. It has somewhere to grow. GitHub issue #449 settled that masking
//      DAIF.I is a giant lock as far as interrupt latency is concerned --
//      right today with one core and no nested interrupts, wrong once
//      interrupts have priorities. The fix is a per-lock LEVEL raising
//      ICC_PMR_EL1 instead, which is what NetBSD's IPL is. Adding
//      `private level: usize` here is a one-place change; adding it to a
//      bare `usize` lock word would have been a change at every call site.
//
// Current limitations (2026-08-28). Keep this list current as call sites
// change; a limitation that goes stale here is worse than none, since it
// invites relying on a guarantee that quietly stopped holding.
// - ONE CLASS, AND EVERY LOCK IS IN IT. GitHub issue #449 decided that
//   whether a handler may take a lock is a property OF THE LOCK, and that
//   the distinction needs somewhere to live before there are two classes.
//   There is one today: every Mutex masks interrupts, so every Mutex is
//   interrupt-takeable. Nothing here records or checks a class, because
//   there is nothing yet to distinguish.
// - NO LEVEL. See point 3 above. Acquiring masks every interrupt, not the
//   ones that could contend, so interrupt latency is bounded by the
//   LONGEST critical section in the kernel rather than by the ones a given
//   handler could actually collide with.
// - NO OWNER, NO RECURSION COUNT. Taking the same Mutex twice on one core
//   hangs and this cannot tell that from waiting for another core. Lock
//   ORDER across call sites is checked by
//   linux_user/intrusive_pool/pool_lock_check.tkb, in the tier that can
//   afford to look.
// - THE GUARD IS THE CALLER'S. mutex_acquire hands back the interrupt
//   state and nothing here makes returning it mandatory. What makes it
//   mandatory is the caller holding it in a linear struct -- see
//   IntrusivePoolGuard, and the guards issue #451 converts.
use "kernel/lib/spinlock.tkb";

// One field, at offset 0, which is what makes `m as usize` the address of
// the word the atomic operates on. `--emit-struct-layout Mutex` says so.
struct Mutex { private word: usize; }

// mutex_irq_save/mutex_irq_restore are supplied by the FILE LIST, the same
// way pool_lock_acquire is (GitHub issue #364). The kernel gives them
// DAIF; linux_user, which has no interrupt mask to save, gives them
// nothing to do. Keeping the mask outside this file is what lets the
// Mutex itself be compiled and exercised host-natively -- and it is the
// same boundary, not a second one.

// Free. Call once, before the Mutex is reachable by anything else.
fn mutex_init(m: *Mutex @ id) {
    // Every function below reaches the word through `m as usize`, which is
    // the word's address only because the word is the first field. That is
    // an accidental-looking property with a real reader, so it is checked
    // rather than assumed (GitHub issue #396's shape, and the sort of thing
    // a later field added at the top would silently break).
    static_assert(offsetof(Mutex, word) == 0,
                  "Mutex.word must be first: `m as usize` is the atomic's address");
    m.word = 0;
}

// Takes it, and returns the interrupt state a matching release must
// restore: 1 if IRQs were already masked, 0 if they were enabled.
//
// MASK, THEN TAKE. The other order leaves a window where this core holds
// the lock and an interrupt can arrive, and a handler wanting the same
// lock then waits for a holder that cannot run until the handler returns
// -- a hang, not a delay. That ordering is the entire reason an interrupt
// handler may take one of these at all (GitHub issue #449).
fn mutex_acquire(m: *Mutex @ id) -> usize !{unsafe} {
    let flags: usize = mutex_irq_save();
    spin_lock(m as usize);
    return flags;
}

// Gives it back, then restores what the matching acquire found. Enables
// ONLY if this acquire is the one that masked, which is what makes the
// pair correct inside an interrupt handler (DAIF.I is already set on
// entry, so the release leaves it set) and correct when two are nested.
fn mutex_release(m: *Mutex @ id, flags: usize) !{unsafe} {
    spin_unlock(m as usize);
    mutex_irq_restore(flags);
}

// One attempt, without masking. For a caller that has something else to do
// rather than wait -- and for proving the exclusion single-threaded, which
// is the only way to observe it without a second core.
fn mutex_trylock(m: *Mutex @ id) -> bool !{unsafe} {
    return spin_trylock(m as usize);
}

fn mutex_is_held(m: *Mutex @ id) -> bool !{unsafe} {
    return spin_lock_is_held(m as usize);
}
